## Provenance Notes by Release

v3.0.2 (Spiral Covenant) — bug-fix release
  - All code in this release is original to this repository. No upstream work
    was ported.
  - Both defects were located from field reports by W3dgraf, credited above.
    The BraiinsOS fix follows the approach proposed in issue #20, applied to
    every BraiinsOS call site: dashboard, Sentinel monitoring, and Sentinel
    miner control, which the report did not cover. The pool-list parsing fix
    also covers the dashboard's pool-configuration path, which had the same
    dictionary assumption.
  - The remaining BraiinsOS fixes were found in-house, by auditing every
    BraiinsOS code path against the Braiins OS Public REST API spec (v1.8.0)
    while proving the issue #20 fix: Automation-page logins ignored by
    monitoring, Settings saves erasing device logins, stale tokens after a
    miner reboot, null-object crashes, nominal-instead-of-measured hashrate,
    blind pool-group creation on a failed read, the off-spec pool payload, the
    201 and bare-boolean responses, the `power_stats` NameError, and the
    missing Sentinel pool URL/worker. No external reporter.
  - The Vnish fixes were found in-house the same way, against Vnish's own
    OpenAPI document (api-doc.json as served by the firmware, for 1.2.6 and
    1.3.4, read from the copies kept in 256foundation/asic-rs under meta/vnish/).
    pyasic's and asic-rs's Vnish clients were read for comparison only. Nothing
    from either project, and no part of the document, is included in this
    repository. No external reporter.
  - The DigiByte Core 9.26.7 move follows the upstream release
    (DigiByte-Core/digibyte v9.26.7); its digests were checked against GitHub's
    asset digests, the release SHA256SUMS and a local sha256sum of the tarball.
  - The reports were reviewed for personal information before being acted on.
    By the reporter's own statement, and as checked, they include no IP
    addresses, wallet addresses, passwords or API tokens.

v2.7.1 (Spiral Citadel) — reporting and celebration release
  - All code in this release is original to this repository. No upstream work
    was ported.
  - The Avalon LED celebration defects and the Sentinel auto-restart cooldown
    fix were found in-house, not from a field report. The LED work began with
    the operator's own observation of an Avalon showing plain white through a
    live block celebration; the six defects behind it, and the seventh in the
    dashboard's manual test endpoint, were found by auditing that path. The
    auto-restart cooldown defect — a restart timestamp recorded only on success,
    so an un-restartable miner was retried every monitor cycle — was found in
    the same audit and is unrelated to any reported symptom.
  - The ZMQ alerting defect — a Prometheus gauge registered and exported but
    never written, so Sentinel raised a false "ZMQ CONNECTION LOST" on every
    start and then latched, masking the real thing — was found by the operator
    reporting that alert on a healthy pool, and traced from there. No external
    reporter.
  - The celebration LED defects were found from live cgminer API output during
    that investigation: an Avalon Nano3s answering Code=14 to the plain-text
    "version" and "stats" the discovery probe and LED reader send, while
    answering the JSON form and accepting ledset normally. The operator's own
    celebrate.log supplied the proof, and corrected a wrong first diagnosis:
    the LED was not stranded by a host dying mid-celebration, as initially
    concluded, but by a celebration that ended cleanly with no saved state to
    restore. The dark-when-idle behaviour was requested by the operator.
    No external reporter.
  - The test-endpoint, auto-restart, ZMQ alerting and celebration LED fixes
    were applied in place on this tag after the release commit, and are
    recorded in the changelog under this version rather than a new one, per the
    project's patch-in-place policy.
  - The per-coin statistics defects were located from a field report by
    Pencreus, credited above. The reported correlation (only coins that had
    never taken a dedicated-port connection were affected) turned out to be
    confounded rather than causal, but the evidence supplied was what allowed
    the real predictor, daemon readiness at smart-port startup, to be
    identified and separated from it.
  - The report was reviewed for personal information before being acted on.
    The API payloads it included carry pool-level statistics only: no wallet
    addresses, worker names or network addresses were present.

v2.7.0 (Spiral Citadel) — Bitcoin chain-identity release
  - The BIP-110 / RDTS chain-split remediation that this release is named for
    is original to this repository, not ported: the daemon pinning and checksum
    gates, the stratum chain gate (internal/daemon/chainguard.go), the Sentinel
    chain_identity alert, the dashboard verdict row, and the coin-upgrade.sh
    reconsiderblock repair path.
  - Upstream work DOES ship in this release: Kamakhu's MinerClassS19, adopted
    from that fork during this cycle and credited in full above. Verified
    against the shipping code: InitialDiff 24000 / MinDiff 16384 /
    MaxDiff 65536 in spiralrouter.go, and classifyDevice() routing
    "antminer s19" in devicehints.go.
  - The adopted commit was reviewed for personal or health information before
    merge; none was present in the code that was taken.

